anyone who's taken even a few taxi rides  in Germany is sure to have ridden in an  eClass Mercedes as the taxi drivers  favorite model a good many of the more  than 13 million a classes that Mercedes  has sold to date have served as cabs  they have a reputation as being highly  robust some are even said to have  clocked up 700,000 kilometers or more  what sex change a scenery that goes to  Portugal where the weather is noticeably  warmer and where Mercedes is presenting  its new generation eClass car testa  Sacha Kopp realizes that many viewers  will be reminded of a taxi and in this  model the driver quickly starts feeling  more like a passenger but the question  is what the driver really want that  Mercedes is w213 series the latest  generation is a completely new model not  just a facelift at 4 meters 92 it's even  a bit longer than its predecessor it was  launched on the European market with  three engine options a 135 kilowatt  gasoline-powered and two diesel engines  we tested the smaller diesel the e 220 D  model with 143 kilowatts of power it  takes seven point three seconds to hit  100 km/h from zero with fuel consumption  rated at four point three liters per 100  kilometers the lower rpms make for less  noise in the dual acoustic glass for  even less at extra cost of course also  new as the drive pilot assisted driving  system it doesn't drive the car but  supports the still active driver Sascha  is going to try out the cars semi  autonomous driving system it's available  up to 210 km/h on the highway but it's  supposed to work for city driving as  well 210 km/h isn't allowed in Portugal  so he'll start off a bit more leisurely  he pulls this lever on the steering  column twice to switch the system on he  can take both feet off the pedals and  even let go of the wheel and it actually  stays in the lane on its own the drivers  should keep both hands on the wheel  after all he's in control not Mercedes  the driver can set the distance to other  cars on the road with the same lever the  drive pilot uses sensors and cameras to  detect road markings and other cars so  even in stop-and-go traffic in town the  Mercedes can pace the car in front as if  on an invisible towline the system can  recognize and keep the lane without  markings up to 130 km/h Sascha explains  that the new drive pilot also supports  automatic lane changing this means the  driver has the option of changing lanes  simply by activating the turn signal  lever all you have to do is hold it for  two seconds in the direction you want to  go and the car itself carries out the  maneuver  the driver may not have even seen the  overtaking car but the driver will have  already had a fix on it and brakes off  its own passing maneuver and the driver  can try again right away by holding the  lever for two seconds well that's what I  you light it another function made  possible by the interaction between the  traffic scientists is cameras and the  drive pilot is the speed limit pilot an  idea originally introduced by Ford the  system automatically reads speed limits  and reduces the speed of necessary such  equips the one thing this Mercedes  probably won't do is pay any speeding  tickets the driver might get my god  driver stops reacting at all the car  will sound an alert quickly bring itself  to a stop and turn the flashers on but  it won't leave the lane by itself  because it has no way of knowing what  the surface there is like and if the car  started swerving it could be a real  problem  sascha points out that the drive pilot  does not come cheap it runs about 2,800  euros on top of the price of an eClass  it would take a lot of taxi driving to  cover the added cost in the cabin a  screen resembling an outsized iPhone  takes up almost the entire instrument  panel sasha explains the mercedes is now  moving away from a dash mounted display  to an integrated two component display  one for the instruments and one for  navigation both are on a twelve point  three inch screen and it's very tempting  to try to adjust something that only  leaves grease spots the actual switches  are built into the steering wheel plus a  touchpad and a dial but there are so  many shiny surfaces on the console that  you need a rag handy to wipe the dust  and fingerprints away after a while that  gets to be something of a pain the bad  news is that neither the sat-nav nor the  digital displays are standard the base  version comes with a conventional round  instruments it feels like having a tube  TV set instead of a flat screen  Daimler's reasoning is that it has to  offer a stripped-down bass version for  the taxi drivers observes that no matter  who drives this e-class there are  occasions when you need to take more  than one passenger and then you need  space in the back especially if it's a  taxi but unlike what Sasha was expecting  the back seat doesn't have that much  room the 10th generation may have added  7 centimeters to its wheelbase but they  don't seem to have gone into more  legroom his knees are pressed against  the front seats and the Headroom is less  than adequate  you know Sasha sums up saying the new  e-class --is active steering takes some  getting used to at high speeds the  driver has to build a level of trust in  the car for him personally the starting  price is a bit too steep especially  since it includes none of the high-tech  gadgetry he's hoping the drive pilot  will eventually find its way into other  Mercedes models then he might find one  right for him
 

